"Virtual Threads are lightweight threads introduced in Java 21. Unlike traditional threads, they are managed by the JVM instead of mapping one-to-one with OS threads. When a virtual thread waits for I/O, such as a database or REST API call, the JVM releases the underlying OS thread so it can execute other tasks. This lets us handle a much larger number of concurrent requests with simple synchronous code, making virtual threads ideal for I/O-heavy Spring Boot microservices."
Suppose my Spring Boot service receives 1,000 requests, and each request calls a mainframe API that takes one second to respond. With traditional threads, many OS threads remain blocked waiting for the response. With virtual threads, while one request waits for the mainframe, the JVM frees the underlying OS thread to process another request. This allows the application to handle many more concurrent requests using the same hardware.
A virtual thread is created and managed by the JVM. It is scheduled to run on a platform thread when it needs CPU time. If the virtual thread performs a blocking I/O operation, the JVM suspends it, releases the platform thread to do other work, and later resumes the virtual thread on any available platform thread.


The QUERY method is the perfect middle ground: It allows a request body (like POST) so you can send complex JSON search filters, but it is safe and cacheable (like GET) because the server knows you are only reading data, not altering it.
